et rows root)\n   343\t        [_ _ _ [operand-id] _] (get rows cons-id)]\n   344\t    (is (= test-id alt-id operand-id)\n   345\t        \"independently built identical subtrees mint one id\")\n   346\t    (is (= 1 (count (filter #(= :literal (second %)) (vals rows)))))\n   347\t    (is (= 4 (count rows)) \":if, :application, :variable, one :literal\")\n   348\t    (is (= ast (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast bc)))))\n   349\t\n   350\t\n   351\t(deftest semantic-bytecode-strips-and-saturates\n   352\t  (is (= (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode (lit 1))\n   353\t         (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode\n   354\t           {:type :literal, :value 1, :tail? true, :eid -5, :yang/pos [1 2]})))\n   355\t  (is (= (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode (app (local 'f) [] false))\n   356\t         (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode {:type :application,\n   357\t                                     :operator (local 'f)})))\n   358\t  (is (= (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode {:type :lambda,\n   359\t                                     :params '[x],\n   360\t                                     :body (lit 1)})\n   361\t         (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode {:type :lambda,\n   362\t                                     :params '[x],\n   363\t                                     :macro? true,\n   364\t                                     :body (lit 1)})))\n   365\t  (is (= {:type :vm/gensym, :prefix \"id\"}\n   366\t         (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode\n   367\t                                      {:type :vm/gensym}))))\n   368\t  (is (= {:type :stream/make, :buffer vm/default-stream-capacity}\n   369\t         (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode\n   370\t                                      {:type :stream/make})))))\n   371\t\n   372\t\n   373\t(deftest malformed-row-sets-each-name-exactly-their-own-rule\n   374\t  (doseq [[rule bc] malformed/malformed-row-sets]\n   375\t    (testing rule\n   463\tOrder is by dependency; U1, U2, U3 are mutually independent; U4 needs U2;\n   464\tU5 needs U4; U6 needs U4 and U5; U7 needs U2.\n   465\t\n   466\t**U1 — walker `:stream/close` (§3.2).** An `:eval-stream-close-source`\n   467\tframe shaped as `:eval-stream-cursor-source` (`ast_walker.cljc:319-326`),\n   468\traising `{:effect :stream/close :stream ref}` through `engine/handle-effect`.\n   469\tCriteria: a parity case in `parity_test.cljc` closing a stream on both\n   470\tevaluators; `Testing yin.vm.parity-test` in the Node output. Size: an\n   471\tafternoon.\n   472\t\n   473\t**U2 — the §7.4 validator and the malformed-row corpus.** A public\n   474\t`yin.vm/validate-rows` (name per implementer) returning nil or the\n   475\tfirst defect `{:rule r :path p}` (or `:id` for an unreached row), rules in\n   476\t§7.4's order, each assuming the earlier held, mirroring `code/well-formed?`.\n   477\t`:slot-kind` gains `node` (`jing/segment-address?`), `data`/`key`\n   478\t(`plain-data?`, moved from `linearize` to a shared home so item 2's one\n   479\tdefinition is one function), `sym`, `kw`, `str`, `int`. `:root-reachable`\n   480\tand an explicit `:acyclic` are added; `semantic-bytecode->ast` calls the\n   481\tvalidator first and keeps its own address check. A published set of\n   482\tmalformed row sets, one per rule, lives beside the corpus. Criteria: every\n   483\trule has a malformed case that names exactly it; `semantic-bytecode-round-trip-law`\n   484\textended to `rows → map → rows` over every program in\n   485\t`parity_test.cljc`'s corpus and over `yang/compile` of the REPL corpus\n   486\t(the map side of the law holds only for canonical maps, so yang output is\n   487\ttested in the rows direction and after one projection); the numeric-key\n   488\tprogram stays. Size: two to three days including the corpus.\n   489\t\n   490\t**U3 — the walker's row loader.** `ast-walker/vm-load-rows` = U2's\n   354\t### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\n   355\t\n   356\tExclusion is a rule of the projection boundary, the same rule §2.4 states\n   357\tfor saturation: these facts are stripped map→rows and never re-derived\n   358\trows→map. The map AST a frontend emits carries them; no row does:\n   359\t\n   360\t- **Source positions** and any frontend metadata (`:yang/*` keys, Clojure\n   361\t  reader metadata on symbols). Tracked instead in secondary side tables.\n   362\t  **Parameter and variable names are not an exclusion**: the boundary\n   363\t  projection retains them in the canonical tuples (`:lambda params`,\n   364\t  `:variable name`), since this design preserves named variables across\n   365\t  the boundary.\n   366\t- **`:macro?`** and `:phase-policy`. `yang.clojure` sets them on defmacro\n   367\t  lambdas (`clojure.cljc:436-438`) and the codec persists `:yin/macro?`\n   368\t  (`v2.cljc:419-420`, schema `v2.cljc:312`). No evaluator reads them\n   369\t  (`ast_walker.cljc:20-23`). The expander needs the fact that a particular\n   370\t  definition is a macro; that fact travels beside the tree as an\n   371\t  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\n   372\t- **`:eid`** (§4.4).\n   373\t- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\n   374\t  the tree names the root. No root fact exists in a row — the root row's\n   375\t  id is the root fact (§4.1).\n   376\t- **`t`, `m`, provenance.** Every provenance fact the codec or linearizer\n   377\t  writes today (`:yin.code/source`, `:yin.code/derived-from`) becomes a\n   378\t  ledger record (§8) or a side-table entry.\n   379\t\n   380\t**Occurrences.** Content identity names *what*; three further things\n  1095\tA tuple loader validates before it decodes, and validation is the §2 table\n  1096\tplus the structural rules a segment needs, nothing else. Both checkers\n  1097\tmirror `code/well-formed?` (`code.cljc:155-180`): each returns nil or the\n  1098\tfirst defect, rules run in order, and each rule may assume the earlier ones\n  1099\theld.\n  1100\t\n  1101\t### 7.4 Row rules\n  1102\t\n  1103\tDefects name a path from the root row (§2.5), or the row's own id for a\n  1104\trow nothing reaches.\n  1105\t\n  1106\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1107\t| Rule              | Defect when                                                                                                                                       |\n  1108\t+===================+===================================================================================================================================================+\n  1109\t| `:tag`            | the element after the id is not a tag of §2.3                                                                                                     |\n  1110\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1111\t| `:arity`          | the count differs from the tag's body arity plus one                                                                                              |\n  1112\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1113\t| `:slot-kind`      | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not an address, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of addresses, a `data` or     |\n  1114\t|                   | `key` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`, a `sym` slot that is not a symbol, a `syms` slot that is not a vector |\n  1115\t|                   | of symbols                                                                                                                                        |\n  1116\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1117\t| `:saturation`     | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil                                                                                                                    |\n  1118\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1119\t| `:id-resolves`    | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set                                                                             |\n  1120\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1121\t| `:acyclic`        | following child ids from a row revisits a row already on its own path                                                                             |\n  1122\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1123\t| `:root-reachable` | a row of the loaded set is not reachable from the root row                                                                                        |\n  1124\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1125\tThe three reference rules are the row counterpart of §7.5's\n  1126\t`:target-bounds`: a row's child slots are references, and they must\n  1127\tresolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n  1128\tmust land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n  1129\taddress check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n  1130\tids, so a cycle would break the hash (§4.1) — and the rule stands\n   312\t          