{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"## Review verdict: REQUEST CHANGES\n\nU1’s implementation is correct, but U2 has two contract-level defects. The required U1 parity test is also incomplete.\n\n### Defects\n\n- **P1 — `validate-rows` does not honor the specified rule ordering.**  \n  [vm.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:812) validates depth-first, applying every rule to one row before visiting the next. Consequently, a later `:id-resolves` defect can mask an earlier `:tag` defect elsewhere. A reproduction returned:\n\n  ```clojure\n  {:rule :id-resolves, :path [0 0]}\n  ```\n\n  despite another reachable row having an invalid tag. Unreachable malformed rows are similarly reported only as `:root-reachable`. This violates the requirement that rules execute globally in §7.4 order and each rule may assume all earlier rules held. Validation needs ordered passes across the loaded row set, followed by graph checks.\n\n- **P1 — structural defect paths use the wrong slot coordinates.**  \n  [vm.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:790) enumerates slots with `(range)`, producing slot indices starting at zero. Section 2.5 defines paths using row-body positions, where the tag is position zero and slots begin at one. Thus the reproduction above should report `[1 1]`, not `[0 0]`. `nodes` edges likewise need pairs such as `[2 0]`, not `[1 0]`.\n\n  The tests missed this because they discard `:path` and compare only `:rule` at [vm_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm_test.cljc:373). The malformed corpus should assert the complete defect map.\n\n- **P1 — the U1 acceptance test neither covers both evaluators nor proves that closing occurred.**  \n  [parity_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/parity_test.cljc:202) runs only the AST walker and asserts only that the result is `nil`. It would pass even if the close effect were never executed. The plan explicitly requires a parity case closing a stream on both evaluators. Add walker and semantic-VM execution, assert both results, and verify observable closure—for example, a subsequent read returns the closed outcome or a put is refused.\n\n- **P2 — new slot-kind validation lacks focused negative coverage.**  \n  [malformed_rows.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/malformed_rows.cljc:35) exercises only an invalid `:str` slot for the new validator. The newly required `node`, `data`/`key`, `sym`, `kw`, and `int` checks are not directly tested; prior tests cover only some other kinds.\n\n### Verification\n\n- JVM: **1266 tests, 165114 assertions — passed**\n- Node/CLJS: **1190 tests, 35032 assertions — passed**\n- Node output included `Testing yin.vm.parity-test`.\n- Automated diff scan found no security or general code-quality smells.\n\nThe U1 frame and effect handling at [ast_walker.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:320) match the design. U2’s centralized `plain-data?`, explicit graph checks, and validator-before-reconstruction integration are directionally sound, but the ordering and path defects prevent sign-off."}}
